DERIVED={
1:('1','The introduction explicitly separates a rigid-link joint chain from a mobile differential-drive system controlled by wheel velocities.'),
2:('1','Link-attached frames move as the mechanism articulates. Kinematics describes that geometry, not the forces or torques causing motion.'),
3:('1','Joint configurations are inputs and Cartesian tool poses are outputs, so this is forward rather than inverse kinematics.'),
4:('1','The stated FK role maps joint-configuration commands into Cartesian observations; it does not optimize the policy or replace a torque model.'),
5:('1,2,3','The source lists collision geometry, differentiable trajectory optimization and link geometry inside simulators. It calls FK relevant to sim-to-real but does not guarantee successful transfer with no mismatch.'),
6:('1','Configuration DOF counts independent joint coordinates, not matrix entries or merely three position coordinates.'),
7:('1','An unconstrained spatial rigid-body pose has three translational plus three rotational freedoms; the planar 2+1 alternative describes a different task space.'),
8:('1','A revolute joint permits rotation about its fixed axis; a prismatic joint permits translation along its fixed axis. Each has one independent coordinate here.'),
9:('1','The slide numbers the immobile base 0, the first moving link 1 and the free-end link n.'),
10:('1','A joint connects links while restricting allowable relative motion. It neither releases all constraints nor fixes every joint against all motion.'),
11:('1','The definition is direct surface contact between the two bodies; point-only contact is not the lower-pair definition supplied.'),
12:('1','The visible labels give cylindrical P+R two freedoms, planar P-P-R three, and spherical R-R-R three.'),
13:('1','For the explicitly assumed fixed-lead screw, axial displacement is determined by rotation; the two visible kinds of motion are coupled, unlike the independent cylindrical P+R coordinates.'),
14:('1','The planar block can translate twice and rotate once; the ball-and-socket has three rotations. These are the pictured three-freedom mechanisms.'),
15:('1','The four named parameters are link length a, twist alpha, offset d and joint angle theta.'),
16:('1','DH systematically assigns frames and derives serial-link transforms. It is not a claim that inverse kinematics is unique or all poses reachable.'),
17:('1','A rigid link fixes the relationship between its supported axes through their common-normal distance a and twist alpha.'),
18:('1','The straight a_(i−1) line has perpendicular markers at both joint axes. It is not the curved body length or base-to-tool reach.'),
19:('1','The displayed connection description names d and theta; a and alpha describe the link’s supported-axis geometry instead.')}
DERIVED.update({
20:('1','The table explicitly defines a_i along X_i and alpha_i about X_i. One is a length and the other an angle.'),
21:('1','d_i is the separation of the X_(i−1) and X_i common-normal directions measured along Z_i; theta_i is their relative rotation about Z_i.'),
22:('1','The frame-i to frame-(i−1) matrix uses preceding-link geometry a_(i−1), alpha_(i−1), together with current d_i and theta_i. The table’s generic link index is not a reason to replace all indices by i.'),
23:('1','The visible factorization is Rx(alpha_(i−1)) Dx(a_(i−1)) Rz(theta_i) Dz(d_i). Reordering the x- and z-axis operations generally changes the result.'),
24:('1','Dx contributes (a,0,0); the final z translation is rotated by Rx into (0,−d sin(alpha),d cos(alpha)). Rz does not change its own z-axis direction. Sum gives (a,−d sin(alpha),d cos(alpha)).'),
25:('1','Row 3 of Rx is (0,sin(alpha),cos(alpha)); column 2 of Rz is (−sin(theta),cos(theta),0). Their dot product is sin(alpha) cos(theta), with alpha=alpha_(i−1), not the printed sin(alpha_i).'),
26:('1','With sin(90°)=1 and cos(90°)=0, translation (a,−d sin(alpha),d cos(alpha)) becomes (2,−3,0) metres.'),
27:('1','Step 3 assigns z_i along the i-th joint axis; x_i follows the common-normal construction instead.'),
28:('1','The stated construction chooses a common perpendicular and puts the origin at its meeting with axis i. It does not use the material midpoint or tool tip. For parallel axes a choice may be nonunique, but the instruction remains a valid construction.'),
29:('1','For the explicitly noncollinear intersecting axes, x_i is normal to their plane. Then y_i completes the right-handed frame; zero common-normal length does not eliminate an axis.'),
30:('1','Step 6 matches frame 0 to frame 1 at zero first joint variable, then chooses terminal origin/x direction to make parameters simple, often zero.'),
31:('1','Row 2 is alpha1=0, a1=L1, d2=0, theta2 variable. L2 belongs to the next link-geometry row.'),
32:('1','The shown axes are parallel and chosen frames have no out-of-plane displacement. Zero alpha/d does not force zero link lengths or zero joint angles.'),
33:('1','Reading the product: translation uses first-link theta1 and second-link theta12; final rotation accumulates theta123.'),
34:('1','The translation column has no theta3, so the frame-3 origin stays fixed when only theta3 changes. The rotation block changes; an offset tool point need not stay fixed.'),
35:('1','theta12=90°−90°=0° and theta123=30°. Position is (2 cos90°+1 cos0°,2 sin90°+1 sin0°,0)=(1,2,0) m, orientation 30°.'),
36:('1','Rotate the local offset (1,0) by +90° to (0,1), then add the frame origin (3,0). The tool is (3,1) m, not the unrotated offset (4,0).'),
37:('1','The table’s first and third revolute joints vary theta1/theta3, while the middle prismatic joint varies d2. The remaining listed geometry is constant.'),
38:('1','The visible second row fixes alpha1 at 90 degrees and theta2 at zero; third-row d3 is L2, not the variable d2.')})
